+Chumby Classic (``chumby-classic``)
+===================================
+
+The ``chumby-classic`` machine emulates the Chumby Classic, a Wi-Fi
+internet appliance with a 320x240 touchscreen built around the Freescale
+i.MX21 SoC (ARM926EJ-S). It runs the device's own Linux 2.6.16 kernel and
+root disk unmodified, booting them from an image of its NAND flash.
+
+Emulated devices:
+
+- ARM926EJ-S CPU and 64 MiB of SDRAM
+- AITC interrupt controller
+- GPT1-3 timers
+- UART1-4 (UART1 is the console)
+- The crypto processor on UART3, with a settable identity (see
+  `Device identity`_); it holds no keys and refuses key and challenge
+  requests
+- Clock and reset module, PLLs locked at the boot loader's settings
+- GPIO ports A-F
+- Keypad port, used as GPIO: the top button (row 0), headphone detect
+  (row 3), LCD power (column 2) and speaker mute (column 4)
+- CSPI1-3, with a TI TSC2100 touchscreen controller and audio codec on
+  CSPI3
+- The sensor card on CSPI1: a Kionix KXP74 accelerometer and an Atmel
+  AT25080A EEPROM (the "DC ID" EEPROM), selected by GPIOs PD28 and PD27
+  through the card's decoder
+- LCD controller: background plane and graphic window, 16 bpp
+- NAND flash controller with a 64 MiB small-page NAND chip
+- PWM, driving the LCD backlight: the screen is dimmed by its duty cycle
+- USB host controller with a three-port full-speed root hub: control,
+  bulk, interrupt and isochronous transfers, so QEMU's USB devices
+  (``usb-storage``, ``usb-kbd``, ``usb-mouse``, ``usb-tablet``,
+  ``usb-audio``, ``usb-hub``...) can be plugged in with ``-device``
+- DMA controller: 16 channels, linear, 2D and FIFO transfers, repeat
+  (chaining) and the bus error status; the timeouts are not modelled
+- SSI1 and SSI2 (as slaves of the codec, in normal, network and I2S
+  modes, with their FIFOs, interrupts and DMA requests) and the audio
+  multiplexer's data routing
+- Watchdog: the stock kernel's ``chumby_sense1`` driver arms it for 2.5
+  seconds and services it every tick, and ``reboot`` resets the machine
+  through it. A missed service performs QEMU's watchdog action
+  (``-action watchdog=...``, a reset by default). The reset status
+  register reports power-on, software, watchdog or (after
+  ``system_reset``) external reset, which the guest shows in
+  ``/proc/resetReason``
+- RTC, counting days, hours, minutes and seconds from ``-rtc base=`` (by
+  default the host's time) on the ``-rtc clock=`` clock, with its alarm,
+  stopwatch and periodic interrupts. The stock kernel has no driver for it
+- I2C controller, with nothing on its bus (the stock kernel has no driver
+  for it either). I2C devices can be added to ``i2c-bus.0`` with
+  ``-device``
+
+Missing devices:
+
+- SDHC: the Chumby Classic has no SD card slot, and its kernel
+  registers no SDHC driver; its registers read back what was last
+  written
+- The USB OTG block's function (device) side, HNP and its transceiver
+  I2C master
+
+Boot options
+------------
+
+The machine boots a Linux kernel directly, as the Chumby's boot loader
+does: ``-kernel`` takes a zImage, which is entered with machine type 851
+(MX21ADS), the type the Chumby's kernel is built for. Without
+``-append``, the kernel gets the stock command line for the first root
+disk::
+
+  console=ttyS0,38400 root=/dev/mtdblock5 rootfstype=cramfs init=/linuxrc chumby_hwversion=3.8
+
+where ``3.8`` is the machine's ``hwversion``.
+
+The NAND flash is a raw image given with ``-drive if=mtd``. Its layout is
+that of ``nanddump``: 131072 records of 528 bytes, each a page's 512 data
+bytes followed by its 16 spare bytes, 69206016 bytes in all. Programs and
+erases are written back to the image unless the drive is read-only or a
+snapshot.
+
+Running
+-------
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+  $ qemu-system-arm -M chumby-classic -kernel zImage \
+      -drive if=mtd,file=nand.img,format=raw -serial stdio
+
+The pen is the absolute pointer: the left button presses the screen. It
+can also be driven from QMP with ``input-send-event`` (``abs`` events on
+the ``x`` and ``y`` axes, 0 to 32767 across the screen, and the ``left``
+button). ``screendump`` captures the screen.
+
+USB devices attach to the host controller's root hub, for example a
+disk image as a USB stick and a keyboard::
+
+  -drive if=none,id=stick,file=stick.img,format=raw \
+  -device usb-storage,drive=stick -device usb-kbd
+
+The stock root disk mounts the first partition of a stick, ``sda1``, on
+``/mnt/usb``.
+
+The top button (the "squeeze switch", ``/dev/switch`` in the guest) is the
+machine's ``button`` property: ``qom-set /machine button true`` presses
+it and ``qom-set /machine button false`` releases it. A key also holds it
+while the key is down, from any display's keyboard or from QMP's
+``send-key`` and ``input-send-event``: F12 unless the machine's
+``button-key`` option names another QKeyCode (``button-key=pause``), or
+``none``. Other keys go on to the guest's keyboard. Without one, the
+first key pressed plugs a USB keyboard (``usb-kbd``) into a free port of
+the root hub, as a user would plug one in to type: the keys are held
+until the guest has configured it (for up to 10 seconds), then typed
+into it. The machine's ``usb-keyboard=off`` option turns this off. A
+snapshot taken with the keyboard plugged in plugs it in again as it
+loads, into a machine started with the same command line.
+
+Headphones are plugged in and out with the machine's ``headphones``
+property (``qom-set /machine headphones true``); the guest reads the
+jack's switch in ``/proc/sys/sense1/hpin``.
+
+The guest's backlight level (the PWM's duty cycle, 0 to 65535) dims the
+displayed screen, and turning the LCD's power off blanks it. Until the
+guest first programs the PWM the backlight is full.
+
+The board's lines are reported over QMP: ``query-chumby-state`` returns
+the backlight level, LCD power, speaker mute, headphones and button (and
+the accelerometer's input and the identity, below), and the
+``CHUMBY_BACKLIGHT`` (at most one each 100 ms), ``CHUMBY_LCD``,
+``CHUMBY_SPEAKER_MUTE`` and ``CHUMBY_HEADPHONES`` events report changes.
+
+Sound goes to the audio backend given with ``-audiodev`` and the
+machine's ``audiodev`` property, as 16-bit stereo at the rate the
+TSC2100 is programmed for, with the DAC's volume and mutes; recording
+takes its input from the same backend (silence if it has none). For
+example, to record what the guest plays to a file::
+
+  -audiodev wav,id=snd0,path=out.wav -M chumby-classic,audiodev=snd0
+
+The accelerometer's axes are the machine properties ``accel-x``,
+``accel-y`` and ``accel-z``: the acceleration along each of the part's
+axes in milli-g, -16000 to 16000 (the part reads +/-2.5 g, 819.2 counts
+per g around 2048). They can be given with ``-M`` and set at any time to
+tilt or shake the device::
+
+  { "execute": "qom-set",
+    "arguments": { "path": "/machine", "property": "accel-x", "value": 500 } }
+
+They are the ``x-mg``, ``y-mg`` and ``z-mg`` properties of the part
+itself, ``/machine/accel``. They start at 0, 0 and 1000, 1 g on Z: the
+part lying flat, which is a guess at how the sensor card sits in an
+upright Chumby. The stock driver samples them every jiffy and reports
+the latest sample and a 16-sample average through ``/dev/accel``.
+
+The emulated clock follows the host's. ``-icount shift=auto,sleep=off``
+lets it run ahead while the guest is idle, which boots several times
+faster.
+
+Interactive use
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+For a person using the screen, run without ``-icount``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+  $ qemu-system-arm -M chumby-classic -kernel zImage \
+      -drive if=mtd,file=nand.img,format=raw -serial stdio
+
+Then the guest's clock keeps to wall-clock time, so its animations,
+timers and ``date`` run at their real pace, and the vCPU sleeps in the
+kernel's idle loop (``WFI``), so an idle Chumby costs the host little
+CPU time. With ``-icount shift=auto,sleep=off`` the guest's clock instead
+jumps ahead whenever it is idle, which suits scripted tests but makes
+timers fire early for a person watching, and it keeps a host core busy.
+
+The watchdog runs on the emulated clock, which stands still while the
+machine is paused. Without ``-icount`` that clock also runs on while the
+host is not running QEMU at all (the host sleeping, QEMU stopped by a
+signal or starved of CPU), so after the 2.5 second timeout runs out the
+guest gets another quarter of a second of running time to service the
+watchdog before it resets the machine. ``-action watchdog=none`` (or
+``pause``) turns the reset off, for instance while debugging the guest.
+
+Device identity
+---------------
+
+The machine properties ``guid``, ``serial`` and ``hwversion`` set the
+device's identity. The crypto processor reports all three (``cpi -p``
+prints the GUID, which the stock software uses as the device ID;
+``chumby_version -n`` and ``-h`` the others), and ``hwversion`` goes on
+the default kernel command line. The DC ID EEPROM holds the hardware
+version, as revision ``00.00.MAJOR.MINOR`` (``/proc/DCversion``) and in
+a made-up ``hwvr`` tag (``dcid -o``), and the last 12 characters of the
+serial number. The defaults are a made-up identity:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+  $ qemu-system-arm -M chumby-classic,guid=C40BB1E0-0000-4000-8000-0E4000000001,serial=0000000000000000,hwversion=3.8 ...
+
+The GUID is 32 hex digits as ``XXXXXXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX``,
+the serial number 16 letters or digits, and the hardware version
+``MAJOR.MINOR``; anything else is refused at startup. Give each emulated
+Chumby its own GUID so that it pairs with a server as a separate device.
+
+Frontend interface
+------------------
+
+A frontend (a window, a test harness, a script) controls and watches
+the machine with the names below. They are stable: they will not be
+renamed, removed or changed in meaning without a note in this section.
+New ones may be added, and a client should ignore fields it does not
+know.
+
+Machine properties, given with ``-M chumby-classic,NAME=VALUE``; all but
+the identity can also be read and set at any time with ``qom-get`` and
+``qom-set`` on ``/machine``:
+
+- ``button`` (bool): the top button, true while held
+- ``headphones`` (bool): true while headphones are plugged in
+- ``button-key`` (string): the QKeyCode that holds the top button,
+  ``f12`` by default, or ``none``
+- ``usb-keyboard`` (bool): whether an unheard key plugs in a USB
+  keyboard; on by default
+- ``accel-x``, ``accel-y``, ``accel-z`` (int): the acceleration along
+  each of the accelerometer's axes in milli-g, -16000 to 16000; 0, 0
+  and 1000 by default
+- ``guid``, ``serial``, ``hwversion`` (string): the identity,
+  ``XXXXXXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX``, 16 letters or digits and
+  ``MAJOR.MINOR``; fixed once the machine is created
+
+``query-chumby-state`` returns the button, headphones, acceleration and
+identity together with the board's outputs, so that a frontend can
+resynchronise with one command:
+
+.. code-block:: json
+
+  { "backlight": 65535, "lcd-on": true, "speaker-mute": false,
+    "headphones": false, "button": false,
+    "accel-x": 0, "accel-y": 0, "accel-z": 1000,
+    "guid": "C40BB1E0-0000-4000-8000-0E4000000001",
+    "serial": "0000000000000000", "hwversion": "3.8" }
+
+``backlight`` is the PWM's duty cycle, 0 to 65535; ``lcd-on`` and
+``speaker-mute`` are the lines the guest drives. The events
+``CHUMBY_BACKLIGHT`` (``level``, at most one each 100 ms, the last
+being the final level), ``CHUMBY_LCD`` (``on``),
+``CHUMBY_SPEAKER_MUTE`` (``muted``) and ``CHUMBY_HEADPHONES``
+(``inserted``) report changes as they happen.
+
+Input:
+
+- The pen is QMP's ``input-send-event`` with ``abs`` events on the
+  ``x`` and ``y`` axes, 0 to 32767 across the screen left to right and
+  top to bottom, and the ``left`` button, down while the pen touches.
+  A tap is a press of 100 to 200 ms. Any display's absolute pointer
+  sends the same events.
+- Keys are QKeyCodes, from ``send-key``, ``input-send-event`` or a
+  display's keyboard. F12 (``button-key``) holds the top button; the
+  others go to the guest's USB keyboard.
+
+Output: ``screendump`` with ``"format": "png"`` (or the default PPM)
+writes the 320x240 screen as the LCD controller composes it, dimmed by
+the backlight.
